  ### Step 1: Extract the Hidden File

  Run the following command in your terminal to extract the hidden file from atbash.jpg:

    steghide extract -sf atbash.jpg

  When it asks you for a passphrase, press Enter to leave it blank. This extracts a text file named encrypted.txt containing the ciphertext:

    krxlXGU{zgyzhs_xizxp_05y2z65z}
  ──────
  ### Step 2: Decrypt the Ciphertext (Atbash Cipher)

  The filename atbash.jpg tells you that the ciphertext is encoded using the Atbash Cipher, which simply mirrors the alphabet:

    Normal:   a b c d e f g h i j k l m n o p q r s t u v w x y z
    Reversed: z y x w v u t s r q p o n m l k j i h g f e d c b a

  Using this table, map each letter of krxlXGU{zgyzhs_xizxp_05y2z65z} to its reversed partner (preserving uppercase/lowercase letters, while keeping numbers and
  punctuation unchanged):

  • k → p
  • r → i
  • x → c
  • l → o
  • X → C
  • G → T
  • U → F
  • z → a
  • g → t
  • y → b
  • h → s
  • s → h
  • x → c
  • i → r
  • z → a
  • x → c
  • p → k
  • y → b
  • z → a

  This reveals the decrypted flag:

